Creating a Simulation Object with Animation
Create a Simulation Object with Animation
While you are creating your simulation model, you will also model simulation objects which animate a
certain work process in 3D.
In our example we model our own station in a Frame. We will demonstrate how to:
• Create the Simulation Object
• Add Animations and Interactions to the Simulation Objects
• Test the Station in the Simulation Model
• Fine-tune the Station
Back to Visualizing the Material Flow
Create the Simulation Object
You can create the simulation object that is required for our animatable object.
Proceed as follows:
• Create a new Frame and open it. Open the dialog Edit 3D Properties. Change to the tab Graphics.
Select the check box Show Content to show the objects contained within it.
Creating a Simulation Object with Animation
Plant Simulation Help 10-1039
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Click Show External Graphic Groups 
  on the View ribbon tab to show the default
graphic of the Frame. Delete the graphic in the graphic group named default.
• Click Import Graphics on the Edit ribbon tab to import an appropriate JT file into the graphic group
named default of the Frame. Our file is called Screwdriver.jt. We received it from a colleague.
Create the Simulation Object
10-1040 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Extract the first simulation object: Click the floor plate of the station by pressing the + key on your
keyboard until only the floor plate, and nothing else, is marked as selected. 3D then shows the floor
plate in a yellowish green.
• Click the graphic with the right mouse button and select Make Simulation Object.
Create the Simulation Object
Plant Simulation Help 10-1041
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Select .MaterialFlow.Station as the class. Name the object PartCarrierStation and change the
coordinates of the Object Position until they are located in the center of the screwdriver. Watch how
the white cross lines change their position in the scene window as you change the position of the object
in the dialog.
• Select the trestle that holds the actual screwdriver of our screwdriver station. Notice that when you
select PartCarrierStation, Plant Simulation shows it in a darker green while it shows the other graphics
in a light yellowish green. Dark green denotes that an object is selected. Yellowish green denotes that
a graphic is selected, meaning that you can use the + key to contract the selection along the graphic
structure.
Create a second simulation object. Select .MaterialFlow.Station as the class. Name the object
Trestle and change the coordinates of the Object Position until they are located in the center of
the screwdriver.
