Modeling a Complex Receiving Department
Model a Complex Receiving Department
This sample model demonstrates how to model a complex receiving department in a Frame.
Here three Sources produce three different parts, namely bricks, glass bricks, and brick carriers. The bricks
and the glass bricks are then loaded onto brick carriers and moved to a ParallelStation that stores them
before a TransferStation loads them onto a truck that is produced by a fourth Source.
We will demonstrate how to:
• Insert the Objects Required for Modeling the Source
• Configure the Individual Stations
• Animate Bricks on the Animation Area of the Brick Carrier
• Animate Brick Carriers on the Animation Area of the Truck
• Show the Content of the Frame
Back to Visualizing the Material Flow
Insert the Objects Required for Modeling the Source
First, we will insert the objects, which we need to model our receiving department, into a Frame.
Modeling a Complex Receiving Department
10-1052 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In the second step, we are going to configure the individual objects.
• Create a new Frame in the Class Library of your simulation model. We named our Frame
MySourceTruckLoadedWithBricks.
• Insert three Sources at the left-hand side of the Frame. We use our own class, named MySource, with
a modified 3D graphic. We named our Sources SourceGlassBricks, SourceBricksCarrier, and
SourceBricks. They will produce glass bricks, brick carriers, and normal bricks.
• Insert two AssemblyStations to the right of the Sources. We named them AssemblyGlassBricks and
AssemblyBricks. Each of them places 50 glass bricks or 50 normal bricks onto a pallet each.
• Insert a TransferStation, a short Track, and another Source. This Source, which we named
SourceTrucks, produces the trucks onto which the commissioned pallets are loaded.
• Insert an Interface that connects our receiving department with the other objects in the simulation
model.
• Connect the objects so that they match the screenshot below. Our finished model looks like this:
Note
As the AssemblyStation is connected to two stations, the order in which you connect it with the
predecessors is important. If the assembly process does not work as expected, check the numbering of
the predecessors.
Plant Simulation shows the predecessors and the successors of an object in a tooltip when you drag
the mouse over the respective Connector.
Then, enter the correct number of the predecessor into the text box Main MU from Predecessor of
the AssemblyStation.
Insert the Objects Required for Modeling the Source
Plant Simulation Help 10-1053
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Complex Receiving Department
Configure the Individual Stations
After we have inserted the objects we need to model our receiving department, we are going to configure
the individual stations.
First, we are going to configure the MUs:
• To create the MU representing the truck, duplicate the class .MUs.Transporter and rename it to Truck.
We deleted its graphic and imported the JT file Truck.jt, which we received from a colleague. Position the
graphic in its window as shown below. We changed its size to 6.3 by 2.2 by 1.6 meters.
Configure the Individual Stations
10-1054 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To create the MU representing the brick carrier, duplicate the class .MUs.Container and rename it to
BricksCarrier. We deleted its graphic and imported the JT file BricksCarrier.jt. We changed its size to
1.1 by 1.1 by 0.15 meters and gave it a capacity of 50 parts (X-Dimension of 5 times Y-Dimension of
10).
• To create the MU representing the normal brick and the glass brick, duplicate the class .MUs.Part twice.
We renamed them to Brick and GlassBrick respectively. Both have a size of 0.2 by 0.1 by 0.05
meters.
Configure the Individual Stations
Plant Simulation Help 10-1055
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

For the bricks we are going to use automatically generated graphics instead of importing JT files.
◦ Open their class in a new window and open the dialog Edit 3D Properties.
◦ Change to the tab Transformation and activate the check box Scale Automatically.
◦ For the GlassBrick, also activate Material Active, select a blueish gray as the Diffuse Color, and select
a Transparency factor of 0.6 to make the glass brick semi-transparent.
◦ Activate Material Active for the GlassBrick. Select orange as the Diffuse Color
The automatically created graphics look like this:
Configure the Individual Stations
10-1056 Plant Simulation Help
